A||||8||HAGS||Is it possible to do in one day?||||1||
Z||000000||HAGS||02-02-2001||09:02 AM||||I need to replace my lifters, is it feasible for me to be able to do this in one evening when the biggest thing I've done is check my pushrods?  Right now I have the head gasket and bolts, I'll need to buy everything else on a $300+ budget, what else should I do while im in there?  Cam? others? I may have one evening and one morning to do this work...I might have one person help.<P>I guess I need manifold gasket? rocker arm gasket? uhhh...any suggestions? Im a newbie!||||reg||1
Z||000001||DrRodey||02-02-2001||09:28 AM||||Sugestions....  Take your time, be calm keep everything organized and dont drop anything.  I made that mistake the first time i tried to just crack the top half of an engine.  loose bolts rattling around in an engine are not fun.<P>I could probably be done in one evening (be eveingn i'm assuming you mean 5-10 pm).  The extra person will be more then a help too.<P>------------------<BR>Hi... My names Dan, and i'm addicted to Fieros.<BR>'88 Formula CONVERTIBLE<BR>'85 Coupe<BR>AOL IM = drrodey<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/files/fieroPage.JPG">          <BR> <A HREF="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/HomePlace.html" TARGET=_blank>freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/HomePlace.html</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-02-2001||10:34 AM||||It's possible. Yes you need the upper gaskets. Felpro makes a "top end" gasket set for the engine. Is this a 2.8L V-6? A 2.5L 4-cyl does not require the head to be removed to swap lifters just the intake manifold. After re-reading your post you specified "head" not "heads" The 4-cyl has an access cover under the intake manifold. You just loosen the rockers, remove push rods, then from the side panel you remove the lifters. There is a gasket for the side cover. Originally GM just siliconed it (same with the rocker cover). But the gasket is better at preventing leaks. There is a rubber gasket for the rocker cover too.<BR>The V-6 will take the better part of a night. The 4-cyl should be a few hours.<BR>Camshaft requires engine to be removed since the cam comes out the front and is the length of the block.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||HAGS||02-02-2001||11:13 AM||||Well originally I planned to drive it down to San Antonio for my cousins wedding and fix it the night before the wedding and the morning after then drive to work that night in Austin...they are really strict here about letting you work on your car in the apartment complex and I sure as heck aint taking it to a shop.  But my plans might be foiled by recent vacuum trouble im guessing, stalling occasionally, it heats up real bad at traffic lights but I usually put it in nuetral and rev it up and the temp sinks down...now when I put it in nuetral there is a screech and what sounds like a household vacuum running...I know its pretty loud because the guys begging for coins for the childrens cancer fund all looked my way and everyone in their cars were looking at me... seems like gas makes it worse sometimes...I'll have to give it very little gas or it will sputter and stall... sometimes gas passing the stalling and I get factory acceleration...||||reg||1
